   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the labor law, in relation to establishing a registry of\nworkplace fatalities in the construction industry to record information\npertaining to all incidents under which an employee performing construction\nwork suffers a work-related fatal injury",
    "basePrintNo": "S8828",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8828-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §44, Lab L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8828\n\nSPONSOR: RAMOS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the labor law, in relation to establishing a registry of\nworkplace fatalities in the construction industry to record information\npertaining to all incidents under which an employee performing\nconstruction work suffers a work-related fatal injury\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF THE BILL:\n\nNew York has always been a state that respects and prizes the workforce.\nProactive measures that protect workers' health and safety have histor-\nically been a hallmark of our state's agenda. However, without accurate\ndata, it is difficult to fulfill that agenda. There is little informa-\ntion on workplace fatalities throughout New York, and what information\nexists is often anecdotal or skewed by local policies.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis measure would require the Department of Labor to establish a\npublicly accessible and searchable online registry of workplace fatali-\nties. Coroners would be required to report job-site deaths along with\nbasic information within 72 hours of the declaration of death, and to\nfile a follow-up report with more in-depth information within 90 days.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nUntil recently, the New York City Department of Buildings only consid-\nered deaths that involved violations of the city's construction code to\nbe workplace fatalities. Shockingly, those involving workplace safety\nviolations but not city code violations were not considered. For exam-\nple, when construction worker Alton Louis died at an affordable housing\nproject at 149 Kent Avenue in Brooklyn-a death the federal Occupational\nSafety and Health Administration (OSHA) deemed a workplace fatality the\ndeveloper was not required to report the fatality or any details of the\nincident to the city agencies with which they work. The death was\nneither investigated nor included in the Department of Buildings' tally\nof workplace fatalities.\n\nWith 58 county coroners or medical examiners in New York State (New York\nCity's five counties are consolidated under the Office of the City\nMedical Examiner), any number of variations of what constitutes a work-\nplace fatality may exist. Local municipalities, OSHA and the Federal\nBureau of Labor Statistics at times may all have different statistics on\nthe number of deaths on the job. Without an accurate, centralized regis-\ntry, containing circumstances of workplace deaths accompanied by demo-\ngraphic data on the victims, the legislature cannot tailor remedies to\nprotect those most at risk.\n\nThe construction industry has the most reliable information on workplace\nfatalities, thanks to the New York Committee for Occupational Safety &\nHealth's (NYCOSH) annual report entitled Deadly Skylines. In its 2019\nreport, NYCOSH concluded there were at least 69 fatalities on New York\nconstruction sites in 2017 a rate of one every five days however, due to\ncounty by county variables, that figure is likely higher. The report\nalso concludes that: 86.7% of fatalities occurred on sites where the\nworkers are not represented; that Latino workers are more likely to die\non the job than non-Latinos; and that workers ages 55-to-64 are most\nlikely to be killed at work. In order to determine how to properly\nprotect the most vulnerable employees, the collection and maintenance of\naccurate, current and complete data is essential.\n\nThis task falls on New York State because the federal government has\nabdicated its responsibility in these matters. Of the 69 fatalities\ncited in NYCOSH's report, only OSHA investigated 30, leaving 56% of\nthose workplace deaths uninvestigated. Further, the federal Bureau of\nLabor Statistics will not release data on 2018 fatalities until December\n2019, almost a year later. Therefore, the federal government cannot be\ncounted on as a reliable and timely source of information when New York\nworkers are killed on the job.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred and twentieth day after it\nshall have become a law; provided, however, that any death subject to\nthe provisions of this act that occurs on or after the day on which this\nbill shall have become a law shall be subject to the reporting\nprovisions of this act and shall be reported within thirty days after\nsuch death. Effective immediately, the addition, amendment and/or repeal\nof any rule or regulation necessary for the implementation of this act\non its effective date are authorized to be made and completed on or\nbefore such effective date.\n",
